Message-Driven Application
This application will include three main components:

MessageQueue: A thread-safe queue to store messages.
Producer: A class that produces messages and adds them to the queue.
Consumer: A class that consumes messages and processes them, tracking success and errors.


=============================================================================

MessageQueue Class
The MessageQueue class will be responsible for handling the storage and synchronization of messages between the producer and the consumer.

=============================================================================

Producer Class
The Producer class will generate messages and add them to the message queue.

=============================================================================
Consumer Class
The Consumer class will consume messages from the message queue, process them, and track the number of successful and failed message processes.

==============================================================================

Main Application
The main class will set up the message queue, start the producer and consumer threads, and run the simulation for a specified time.

=================================================================================
Unit Tests
We will use JUnit 5 for unit testing the application. The tests will simulate both successful and failure scenarios.

Maven Dependencies for JUnit 5
If you're using Maven, add the following dependencies to your pom.xml for JUnit 5:

<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
        <artifactId>junit-jupiter-api</artifactId>
        <version>5.7.1</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
        <artifactId>junit-jupiter-engine</artifactId>
        <version>5.7.1</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>


